es independently added the same router entry for a new project \\u2014 duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge, not a mangled-file problem.\\n- Ranking repos by commit-days (not recency) worked: 26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean. Flint is genuinely new work but still does not appear on the active list, so the \\\"newest active repo\\\" pick alone would have chosen the wrong thread \\u2014 Eric's clarification was needed.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Run `smoke_test.py` \\u2014 the TypeSafe API key on the Mac has never been tested.\\n- Build the triage state for the 200 sample files, then write and commit the blind answer key before the first Jev call.\\n- Investigate `perch-pull` failures (9/26\\u20139/27): log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` \\u2014 not yet opened.\\n- `SecondBrain` is still behind GitHub; dev-sync does not cover it. Needs a separate pull.\\n- `RunningTheRoom` is missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24.\\n- Memory compiler is backlogged a few logs; a flush failed 9/27 on a session limit.\\n- Open question for Eric: go to maintenance mode on TypeSafe, or keep testing?\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":358},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b305e52a-cde2-4fc2-8c1a-97b42a769207","timestamp":"2026-09-30T16:29:41.438Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"5aabd1a9-14a6-4a3d-8122-b998ac8c6f10","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b305e52a-cde2-4fc2-8c1a-97b42a769207","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"5ae071b9-77a9-40e8-90af-6052f7e24fd3","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":528},"type":"attachment","uuid":"798f5768-aadb-4081-8e5d-173b4f4b2c03","timestamp":"2026-09-30T16:29:41.609Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"5aabd1a9-14a6-4a3d-8122-b998ac8c6f10","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"798f5768-aadb-4081-8e5d-173b4f4b2c03","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:startup","toolUseID":"5ae071b9-77a9-40e8-90af-6052f7e24fd3","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":530},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d0292005-4b4f-46bd-b5aa-1714295b19b9","timestamp":"2026-09-30T16:29:41.612Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"5aabd1a9-14a6-4a3d-8122-b998ac8c6f10","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d0292005-4b4f-46bd-b5aa-1714295b19b9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["## Today\nWednesday, September 30, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n310 compiled articles (230 concepts, 79 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:46)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac to pull GitHub state from the Windows laptop, then picked up the Flint / Drive-triage thread.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked which repo to resume: TypeSafe_Sandbox (newest active) vs Flint (newer commits but not yet on the active list). Eric clarified the Drive triage work belongs to Flint.\n- Flint's books, OCR and audio exist only on Windows, so atomizing and OCR must run there. The Mac can still run the Drive triage test, because `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` (200-file sample) is in git and Drive is mounted on the Mac.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Resume Flint, with the Drive triage (Jev pilot) executed inside TypeSafe_Sandbox.\n- Router conflict on `RESEARCH.md`: Windows had already added Flint, so the Mac's duplicate line was dropped and the Windows line kept. Resolved by hand, no `.from-*` files created.\n- No Jev API call happens until Claude's blind answer key is written and committed.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Two machines independently added the same router entry for a new project — duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge, not a mangled-file problem.\n- Ranking repos by commit-days (not recency) worked: 26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean. Flint is genuinely new work but still does not appear on the active list, so the \"newest active repo\" pick alone would have chosen the wrong thread — Eric's clarification was needed.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Run `smoke_test.py` — the TypeSafe API key on the Mac has never been tested.\n- Build the triage state for the 200 sample files, then write and commit the blind answer key before the first Jev call.\n- Investigate `perch-pull` failures (9/26–9/27): log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` — not yet opened.\n- `SecondBrain` is still behind GitHub; dev-sync does not cover it. Needs a separate pull.\n- `RunningTheRoom` is missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24.\n- Memory compiler is backlogged a few logs; a flush failed 9/27 on a session limit.\n- Open question for Eric: go to maintenance mode on TypeSafe, or keep testing?\n","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. macOS primary since 2026-08-05; Windows 11 still active. Tool paths: CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE, 5/5. →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 + Sprint Film Check built. NEXT: Tim/Jacob → export; Joe notes → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh\n- **Machine split (9/24)**: Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD. →handoff 2026-09-24-machine-split\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: K-B self-audit done. Ship to a named user q2wk: RI5 #002 (`_v2` only) wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/23)**: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py`. Wk4 COLO: EPA layer only, full profile? →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026/docs/`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/26)**: 013 built; size Q + 011 await Mauro (asst GM). →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **Sleep sched (9/16)**: R1 merged; NEXT night/early-kickoff. →`sleep-scheduler/`\n- **TypeSafe (9/28)**: Drive triage FAIL 76.5%; Jev>Haiku, used for Flint. PR #31 draft; Eric audit. →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/`\n- **Flint (9/28)**: interview done+applied. NEXT Eric: shortlists, primer, CVASPS. →`Flint/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PFF DB (9/22)**: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB. Both: 2026 thru wk3 + video. →`PFF_Data_Project/docs/`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both copies only for shared events; Codex has no prompt-submit event. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a space, repo an underscore.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Win: `Desktop\\DEV` syncs via Drive (robocopy retired 9/22). No large DBs/junctions: renv via `RENV_PATHS_LIBRARY_ROOT`, PFF via `PFF_DB`.\n- Schedules →automation-cadence. CFB capture Mac only. Dashboard →command-centre.\n- Post-merge: delete merged branches/worktrees unasked (verify first) →Tier A post-merge-cleanup.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **OKR gate (9/14)**: AI buy-in + data governance (Anthony = outside-firm adviser, NOT Mauro). NCAA charter or park?\n\n---\n\n## HOOK FAILURES — surface these to the user on the first response\n\n- **memsearch-sync** ×30 (2026-09-28 00:05 → 2026-09-28 17:48 UTC): index ETIMEDOUT 35x consecutively — indexing may not be keeping up: spawnSync memsearch ETIMEDOUT\n- **compiler-stale-lock** ×4 (2026-09-23 01:01 → 2026-09-28 03:46 UTC): reclaimed compile lock held 121min by pid 109324 (compile.py --auto)\n- **compiler-flush-failed** ×10 (2026-09-27 17:32 → 2026-09-28 04:47 UTC): memory-compiler flush failed (session codex-01a0e43b-bff1-71a1-8797-6383c003b75c): **Context:** Force Plate Practitioner course archiving project (Applied Performance community course) — completing the video archive and Zotero library import, continued from a prior Codex session.  * | a single failu…\n- **compiler-backlog** ×3 (2026-09-23 01:01 → 2026-09-25 02:25 UTC): 2 log(s) backlogged, compiling newest 2 this run - run compile.py manually to catch up\n- **daily-backup** ×7 (2026-09-23 07:53 → 2026-09-30 07:51 UTC): 1 error(s): repo-behind-blocked:global-memory - see ~/.claude/scripts/logs/daily-backup.log\n- **perch-pull** ×2 (2026-09-26 16:26 → 2026-09-27 00:34 UTC): error exit=1 after 3 attempt(s) — see /Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json\n- **adjudicate-budget-tripped** ×1 (2026-09-27 16:06 → 2026-09-27 16:06 UTC): stopped after 315,063 / 250,000 tokens\n- **vald-pull** ×1 (2026-09-28 19:02 → 2026-09-28 19:02 UTC): error exit=1 after 2 attempt(s) — see /Users/kristinarash/DEV/ForcePlate_DecisionSystem/logs/last_run.json\n- 10 unparsed line(s); see the file.\n\nFull log: `~/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log`. Surface this once; clear the file after the user has seen it and the cause is handled.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (4335 chars, updated 2026-09-30 11:14)","## Today\nWednesday, September 30, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n310 compiled articles (230 concepts, 79 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:46)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac to pull GitHub state from the Windows laptop, then picked up the Flint / Drive-triage thread.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked which repo to resume: TypeSafe_Sandbox (newest active) vs Flint (newer commits but not yet on the active list). Eric clarified the Drive triage work belongs to Flint.\n- Flint's books, OCR and audio exist only on Windows, so atomizing and OCR must run there. The Mac can still run the Drive triage test, because `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` (200-file sample) is in git and Drive is mounted on the Mac.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Resume Flint, with the Drive triage (Jev pilot) executed inside TypeSafe_Sandbox.\n- Router conflict on `RESEARCH.md`: Windows had already added Flint, so the Mac's duplicate line was dropped and the Windows line kept. Resolved by hand, no `.from-*` files created.\n- No Jev API call happens until Claude's blind answer key is written and committed.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Two machines independently added the same router entry for a new project — duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge, not a mangled-file problem.\n- Ranking repos by commit-days (not recency) worked: 26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean. Flint is genuinely new work but still does not appear on the active list, so the \"newest active repo\" pick alone would have chosen the wrong thread — Eric's clarification was needed.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Run `smoke_test.py` — the TypeSafe API key on the Mac has never been tested.\n- Build the triage state for the 200 sample files, then write and commit the blind answer key before the first Jev call.\n- Investigate `perch-pull` failures (9/26–9/27): log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` — not yet opened.\n- `SecondBrain` is still behind GitHub; dev-sync does not cover it. Needs a separate pull.\n- `RunningTheRoom` is missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24.\n- Memory compiler is backlogged a few logs; a flush failed 9/27 on a session limit.\n- Open question for Eric: go to maintenance mode on TypeSafe, or keep testing?\n","<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Claude Code: `references/claude-code-tools.md`\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n- Muse: `references/muse-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>","\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n  <ctx_commands>\n    \"ctx stats\" | \"ctx-stats\" | \"/ctx-stats\" | context savings question\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\n\n    \"ctx doctor\" | \"ctx-doctor\" | \"/ctx-doctor\" | diagnose context-mode\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx upgrade\" | \"ctx-upgrade\" | \"/ctx-upgrade\" | update context-mode\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\n\n    \"ctx purge\" | \"ctx-purge\" | \"/ctx-purge\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\n\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\"\n  </ctx_commands>\n\n</context_window_protection>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0635e39e-26ae-48fd-b31f-7f74fc942463","timestamp":"2026-09-30T16:29:41.613Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: ## Today\nWednesday, September 30, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n310 compiled articles (230 concepts, 79 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:46)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac to pull GitHub state from the Windows laptop, then picked up the Flint / Drive-triage thread.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked which repo to resume: TypeSafe_Sandbox (newest active) vs Flint (newer commits but not yet on the active list). Eric clarified the Drive triage work belongs to Flint.\n- Flint's books, OCR and audio exist only on Windows, so atomizing and OCR must run there. The Mac can still run the Drive triage test, because `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` (200-file sample) is in git and Drive is mounted on the Mac.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Resume Flint, with the Drive triage (Jev pilot) executed inside TypeSafe_Sandbox.\n- Router conflict on `RESEARCH.md`: Windows had already added Flint, so the Mac's duplicate line was dropped and the Windows line kept. Resolved by hand, no `.from-*` files created.\n- No Jev API call happens until Claude's blind answer key is written and committed.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Two machines independently added the same router entry for a new project — duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge, not a mangled-file problem.\n- Ranking repos by commit-days (not recency) worked: 26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean. Flint is genuinely new work but still does not appear on the active list, so the \"newest active repo\" pick alone would have chosen the wrong thread — Eric's clarification was needed.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Run `smoke_test.py` — the TypeSafe API key on the Mac has never been tested.\n- Build the triage state for the 200 sample files, then write and commit the blind answer key before the first Jev call.\n- Investigate `perch-pull` failures (9/26–9/27): log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` — not yet opened.\n- `SecondBrain` is still behind GitHub; dev-sync does not cover it. Needs a separate pull.\n- `RunningTheRoom` is missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24.\n- Memory compiler is backlogged a few logs; a flush failed 9/27 on a session limit.\n- Open question for Eric: go to maintenance mode on TypeSafe, or keep testing?\n\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash, Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds football GPS and force-plate pipelines. Wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo on 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-first player-coach near Iowa, $90K+ floor, mid-2027. Read `~/DEV/ContextVault` before career or personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python/R. macOS primary since 2026-08-05; Windows 11 still active. Tool paths: CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB. No cloud servers or schedulers without Baylor IT approval. Google Drive only; keep DuckDB local to avoid sync locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n- Staff-facing text: Athletic Performance (not S&C), Athletic Medicine (not sports medicine), Applied Performance (not sport science).\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Challenge vague or rehearsed reasoning: \"sharpened, not soothed.\"\n- Decide standard technical details, state the choice, and move on.\n- Build in small steps. Do not break mid-season work.\n- Authors direction when leadership does not; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo group.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Prioritization slate (9/14)**: LIVE, 5/5. →Tier A prioritization-slate\n- **Status report**: #3 by 9/25, biweekly to 12/18. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: phone check; host or local? →Tier A personal-site-build-routes\n- **RTP CLA (9/26)**: L1–2 + Sprint Film Check built. NEXT: Tim/Jacob → export; Joe notes → L3. →`RTP_Constraints_Course/docs/`\n- **ATH Tech**: shipped; ITS pending; run 9/29. →Tier A tech-inventory-refresh\n- **Machine split (9/24)**: Win=day, Mac=eve; Mac sole writer GPS/Perch/VALD. →handoff 2026-09-24-machine-split\n- **Ship queue (9/25)**: K-B self-audit done. Ship to a named user q2wk: RI5 #002 (`_v2` only) wk 9/28, intern M1 10/9, AP Digest ch1 10/23. →`RunningTheRoom/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **FB matchups (9/23)**: EPA = PFF via `scripts/pff_epa_profile.py`. Wk4 COLO: EPA layer only, full profile? →`MatchupAnalysis_FB2026/docs/`\n- **PP Recruiting (9/26)**: 013 built; size Q + 011 await Mauro (asst GM). →`PP_Recruiting/docs/`\n- **Sleep sched (9/16)**: R1 merged; NEXT night/early-kickoff. →`sleep-scheduler/`\n- **TypeSafe (9/28)**: Drive triage FAIL 76.5%; Jev>Haiku, used for Flint. PR #31 draft; Eric audit. →`TypeSafe_Sandbox/docs/`\n- **Flint (9/28)**: interview done+applied. NEXT Eric: shortlists, primer, CVASPS. →`Flint/docs/STATUS.md`\n- **PFF DB (9/22)**: swap Parquet on Drive, never the DB. Both: 2026 thru wk3 + video. →`PFF_Data_Project/docs/`\n\n## Environment Notes\n- Hooks: patch both copies only for shared events; Codex has no prompt-submit event. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- daily-backup: manifest entries alone copy nothing; sweep nested ~/.claude repos →handoff 09-19.\n- `DEV/Linda Projects` = private I-983 repo; folder has a